Practice Problems — Topic 10
Q1. Pre-power checklist construction:
Create a complete 20-item pre-power checklist for your flyback capstone. For each item include: (1) what you check, (2) how you check it (visual / multimeter setting / calculation), (3) the pass criterion (what value or observation means it is correct).
Your 20 items must include at minimum: isolation resistance test, short circuit check, all polarised component orientation checks, all critical component value verifications, and mechanical integrity checks.
Q2. Bring-up phase sequencing:
A student builds their flyback converter and immediately connects it to the mains on first assembly. It immediately blows the fuse and the MOSFET is destroyed.
(a) What is the correct bring-up procedure the student should have followed?
(b) List five possible causes of immediate MOSFET failure on first mains application
(c) How does a series current-limiting bulb protect against MOSFET destruction during bring-up?
(d) How does a variable bench supply (bypassing the bridge rectifier initially) allow safer diagnosis?
(e) What minimum equipment does a student need to safely bring up a mains-powered converter? Rank in priority order.
Q3. Fault diagnosis exercises:
For each symptom, describe your diagnostic procedure (what you measure, in what order) and the most likely cause:
(a) UC3842 VCC pin is at 0V, no gate drive signal
(b) Gate drive frequency is 47kHz instead of 96kHz
(c) Output voltage is 6V instead of 12V at full load but 12V at no load
(d) Output voltage oscillates slowly between 10V and 14V (feedback instability)
(e) Converter works at no load and 0.5A but MOSFET overheats at 1A load
Q4. Efficiency measurement and analysis:
You measure the following at V_in = 120V DC (bench supply), I_load = 2A:
V_out = 11.93V, I_in measured = 0.248A.
(a) Calculate P_in and P_out
(b) Calculate measured efficiency
(c) Your theoretical prediction was η = 83.8%. What is the discrepancy and is it concerning?
(d) The 0.248A input current seems high. At η = 83.8% and V_in = 120V: I_in_theoretical = P_in/(V_in) = (P_out/0.838)/120. Calculate expected I_in and compare to measured.
(e) List four factors that could cause measured efficiency to be lower than theoretical prediction.
Q5. Complete bring-up report:
Write a brief bring-up report for your flyback capstone project in the format you would upload to GitHub. The report should cover:
(a) Summary of pre-power checks (all 20 items from Q1 — state which passed and which required correction)
(b) Phase 1 results: first power application observations (describe what you expect to see in each measurement)
(c) Phase 2 waveform verification: describe the expected waveforms at each test point and what deviations would indicate problems
(d) Phase 3 load sweep: create a blank table with the correct columns that you would fill in during bring-up
(e) Lessons learned section: write three specific technical lessons you would document for a future engineer building the same converter, based on the most likely problems you might encounter (from your understanding of the design and the common failures discussed in this topic)
